LeanGRPO: Eliminating Redundant Recomputation in Diffusion RL Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2609.03528v1 [cs.LG] 03 Sep 2026 LeanGRPO: Eliminating Redundant Recomputation in Diffusion RL Sijie Wang Zhiqiang Tan Xinrui Yang Shaohuai Shi * Affiliation: School of Computer Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen Affiliation: [0.25em] sijiewang@stu.hit.edu.cn tanzhiqiang@stu.hit.edu.cn Email: xinruiyang@stu.hit.edu.cn shaohuais@hit.edu.cn Affiliation: Code: github.com/coderwayne3025/LeanGRPO Abstract Diffusion reinforcement learning (RL) has recently achieved significant success in post-training image and video generative models. However, most diffusion RL methods, including DanceGRPO and FlowGRPO, recompute selected timesteps with gradient tracking after rollout. Under on-policy training with the same backend for rollout and update, this recomputation is mathematically redundant. Intuitively, the rollout and policy update steps can reuse the same feed-forward backbone to avoid redundant computation, but doing so can incur a large memory overhead during rollout. To address the issue, we present LeanGRPO by restructuring the data-parallel layout and introducing two recompute-free training schedules for trajectory-logprob diffusion RL: (1) LeanGRPO-Retain enables gradient tracking during rollout and directly reuses the resulting computation graphs and saved activations for backward during update, requiring no recomputation; and (2) LeanGRPO-Reweight also enables gradients during rollout, but immediately backpropagates each selected step using a provisional advantage and delays gradient synchronization, then corrects the provisional gradients with the true advantage after the trajectory is completed. These schedules target different model scales and input sizes. Across FlowGRPO/DanceGRPO with FLUX.1-dev and Wan, LeanGRPO achieves up to 1.83 × 1.83	imes end-to-end speedup while preserving the original optimization objective. 1 Introduction Reinforcement learning has recently become an important tool for aligning diffusion and flow-matching generative models with human preferences and task-specific rewards ( Ho et al., 2020 ; Song et al., 2021 ; Lipman et al., 2023 ) . A prominent family of methods formulates the denoising process as a policy trajectory and optimizes terminal rewards through transition log-probabilities. Despite its effectiveness, this trajectory-logprob formulation remains computationally expensive. Representative methods, including DDPO ( Black et al., 2024 ) , DPOK ( Fan et al., 2023 ) , FlowGRPO ( Liu et al., 2025b ) , and DanceGRPO ( Xue et al., 2025b ) , follow the workflow shown in Fig. 1 (a). The model first samples a denoising trajectory without gradients to generate terminal images or videos and compute rewards. After rewards and group advantages become available, the update stage recomputes selected denoising timesteps to reconstruct the differentiable paths required for backpropagation. When the policy remains unchanged between rollout and update and both stages use the same backend, this recomputation is mathematically redundant because the same denoising predictions were already computed during rollout. As detailed in Appendix G , we find that most publicly available diffusion RL implementations exhibit this issue. The most straightforward solution is to enable gradient tracking during rollout. In practice, however, even under on-policy training with the same backend for rollout and update, most existing diffusion RL methods retain the recompute-based design. This is not merely an engineering oversight: terminal advantages become available only after rollout completion and reward aggregation, whereas retaining all selected-timestep computation graphs and their saved activations until then can incur prohibitive memory consumption as the number of selected timesteps increases . Eliminating recomputation is thus feasible in principle, but the central challenge is controlling the resulting GPU memory overhead. Figure 1: Comparison of native diffusion RL training and recompute-free LeanGRPO. Here, c b c_{b} denotes the b b -th conditioning prompt, and ϵ b , m \epsilon_{b,m} denotes the independently sampled initial Gaussian latent noise for its m m -th generation. We therefore introduce LeanGRPO, an objective-preserving systems framework that eliminates redundant update-stage recomputation from existing trajectory-logprob diffusion RL algorithms. We first restructure the data-parallel (DP) layout ( Li et al., 2020 ) used by conventional GRPO. Instead of assigning different prompts to different GPUs and generating multiple samples locally, all GPUs process the same prompt while independently generating different samples. This avoids requiring each GPU to retain the computation graphs and saved activations of multiple samples. On top of this shared-prompt layout, we propose two complementary recompute-free schedules: LeanGRPO-Retain preserves the selected-timestep rollout graphs and directly reuses them for backward once terminal advantages become available (Fig. 1 (b)). It eliminates update-stage recomputation with a simple training schedule, but its activation memory grows with the number of selected timesteps. LeanGRPO-Reweight instead consumes each selected-timestep graph through provisional backward during rollout, materializes a provisional gradient, and releases its saved activations. After sampling completes and the terminal reward becomes available, the provisional gradient is corrected using the corresponding advantage (Fig. 1 (c)). By immediately consuming each selected-timestep graph and its saved activations, this schedule avoids the memory growth caused by their accumulation. The memory cost is instead shifted to retaining a full provisional gradient on each rank until advantage correction recovers the same policy gradient in exact arithmetic. Our contributions are as follows: 1. We identify update-stage recomputation as a major systems bottleneck in trajectory-logprob diffusion RL: it is mathematically redundant under an unchanged policy, yet required to recover discarded gradient paths. 2. We introduce LeanGRPO, a framework compatible with most open-source trajectory-logprob diffusion RL algorithms. It provides two complementary recompute-free training schedules: LeanGRPO-Retain and LeanGRPO-Reweight. 3. We integrate LeanGRPO with multiple diffusion RL algorithms and model backbones. Extensive experiments across different model scales and RL workflows demonstrate that LeanGRPO achieves up to a 1.83 × 1.83	imes speedup over native training. GPU memory profiling further characterizes the applicable scenarios of the two schedules and provides guidance for practical deployment. 2 Related Work Recent work improves reward assignment ( Huang et al., 2026 ) , timestep selection ( He et al., 2026 ) , and sampling efficiency ( Li et al., 2026a ) , but commonly retains the two-stage execution pattern in which rollout is performed without gradients and selected transitions are recomputed during update. LeanGRPO is orthogonal to these algorithmic improvements: it preserves their sampling distributions and policy objectives while changing when differentiable transition information is materialized and consumed. Distributed RL systems such as VeRL-Omni ( Huang et al., 2026 ) and BiDiRL ( Tan et al., 2026 ) optimize resource placement, specialized generation backends, and asynchronous execution. They primarily improve orchestration and data movement across phases rather than eliminate redundant policy evaluations. 3 Preliminaries Trajectory-Logprob Diffusion RL. We consider reinforcement learning for diffusion and flow-matching generative models ( Ho et al., 2020 ; Song et al., 2021 ; Lipman et al., 2023 ) . Given a conditioning