ser et al., 2024 ) , Wan2.1-1.3B and Wan2.1-14B ( Wan et al., 2025 ) , and Wan2.2-TI2V-5B ( Wan Team, 2025 ) . 5.2 End-to-End Training Efficiency Figure 3: End-to-end training efficiency of LeanGRPO on DanceGRPO, FlowGRPO-Fast, MixGRPO-Flash, and FlowGRPO. As shown in Fig. 3 , the main trend is that speedup increases with the timestep fraction. This fraction denotes the proportion of timesteps selected for training. As it increases, the update stage accounts for a larger fraction of the total step time, as does its recomputation. Eliminating this recomputation therefore yields a larger speedup. We next examine hyperparameter settings commonly used in practical training and discuss their effects on speedup. Each method is independently launched three times. We discard the first complete optimizer update as warm-up and report the mean latency over the following three updates for each run. Final results are reported as the mean across the three independent runs. (i) Fine-tuning and precision. Fig. 3 (a)–(c) compares fine-tuning modes and numerical precision. Under BF16 full fine-tuning, Reweight and Retain achieve 1.44 × 1.44	imes – 1.81 × 1.81	imes and 1.18 × 1.18	imes – 1.29 × 1.29	imes speedups, respectively. Reweight achieves higher speedups because it combines the gradient reduce-scatter operations after individual backward passes into a single operation at the end, thereby reducing training time. Under BF16 LoRA ( Hu et al., 2021 ) , the corresponding ranges are 1.26 × 1.26	imes – 1.43 × 1.43	imes and 1.27 × 1.27	imes – 1.42 × 1.42	imes . LoRA reduces the synchronization cost of trainable gradients and therefore narrows the gap between the two variants. With FP32 LoRA, Reweight and Retain achieve 1.27 × 1.27	imes – 1.47 × 1.47	imes and 1.25 × 1.25	imes – 1.46 × 1.46	imes speedups, showing that numerical precision has limited effect on the gains. (ii) Model scaling. Fig. 3 (d)–(f) evaluates LeanGRPO with Wan2.1-1.3B, Wan2.2-5B, and Wan2.1-14B under the same sampling configuration. Across these model scales, Reweight and Retain achieve 1.24 × 1.24	imes – 1.49 × 1.49	imes and 1.22 × 1.22	imes – 1.46 × 1.46	imes speedups, respectively. These results show that the benefit of eliminating update-stage recomputation is preserved as the backbone scales from 1.3B to 14B parameters. (iii) Algorithm generality. Fig. 3 (g)–(i) evaluates LeanGRPO on three additional settings. On FlowGRPO-Fast, MixGRPO-Flash, and FlowGRPO with SD3.5-Medium, Reweight achieves 1.25 × 1.25	imes – 1.67 × 1.67	imes , 1.22 × 1.22	imes – 1.47 × 1.47	imes , and 1.43 × 1.43	imes – 1.83 × 1.83	imes speedups, while Retain achieves 1.19 × 1.19	imes – 1.30 × 1.30	imes , 1.15 × 1.15	imes – 1.29 × 1.29	imes , and 1.12 × 1.12	imes – 1.21 × 1.21	imes , respectively. These results show that LeanGRPO is effective across different timestep-selection and SDE-window strategies. (iv) Multi-node scalability. Fig. 3 (j)–(k) scales DanceGRPO from 4 to 32 GPUs, with FSDP applied within each node. Retain and Reweight maintain stable speedups across different GPU counts. (v) Batched execution. Fig. 3 (l) reports speedups when the micro-batch size is greater than one. 5.3 Time Breakdown Fig. 4 decomposes the end-to-end step time of case (a) and case (b) in Fig. 3 into different stages. Under BF16 full fine-tuning, Native spends 402.45 seconds on recomputation. Retain eliminates this cost while leaving training nearly unchanged (999.16 versus 989.44 seconds). Reweight also delays gradient synchronization, reducing training to 535.15 seconds with 30.33 seconds of synchronization and yielding a 1.81 × 1.81	imes end-to-end speedup. Under BF16 LoRA, recomputation accounts for 411.46 seconds, or 30.1% of the native step. Because LoRA gradients are much smaller, Reweight and Retain have similar step times (956.36 versus 960.69 seconds), and Reweight spends only 0.60 seconds on synchronization. Figure 4: End-to-end step-time breakdown of Native, LeanGRPO-Retain, and LeanGRPO-Reweight on Dance