 and Reweight. The left side of Fig. 5 shows that both LeanGRPO variants preserve an overall reward improvement trend comparable to Native. The right side shows that LeanGRPO achieves higher rewards within the same training time. On Wan2.1-1.3B, Native reaches the target reward in 13.00 hours; LeanGRPO-Retain requires 11.45 hours ( 1.14 × 1.14	imes faster), and LeanGRPO-Reweight requires 9.68 hours ( 1.34 × 1.34	imes faster). On FLUX.1-dev, Native requires 25.45 hours, while LeanGRPO-Retain and LeanGRPO-Reweight reach the target in 17.39 and 17.38 hours, respectively (both 1.46 × 1.46	imes faster). Appendix E complements these curves with direct one-update comparisons: under a strictly deterministic fixed trajectory, Retain is bitwise identical to Native, while Reweight has gradient cosine 0.999802 and 1.99% relative ℓ 2 \ell_{2} error. 5.6 GPU Memory Usage Fig. 6 compares the memory usage of Native, Reweight, and Retain in two settings. Panel (a) considers image generation, where tensors are smaller and per-rank micro-batches commonly exceed one. At a micro-batch size of two, Reweight stores two full gradients per rank and OOMs after backward at the first selected timestep. Retain’s resident memory grows approximately linearly with the number of retained graphs. However, it remains below the optimizer-stage peak, leaving the complete-step peak unchanged. Native retains no graphs across timesteps. Its resident memory therefore remains constant, and its complete-step peak is likewise determined by the optimizer stage. Panel (b) considers video generation, where larger tensors typically restrict each rank to a micro-batch size of one. Retain’s graph and activation memory then grows rapidly, causing OOM at the sixth selected timestep. Reweight instead adds one full gradient per rank. This overhead is independent of the number of selected timesteps, so its memory remains stable, as does Native’s. At the same micro-batch size, Reweight uses more memory and achieves a 1.22 × 1.22	imes speedup. To test whether this gain comes only from using more memory, we increase Native’s micro-batch size to two. Native then reaches a higher peak memory than Reweight but achieves only a 1.03 × 1.03	imes speedup. Thus, increasing the micro-batch size provides little efficiency gain for large-tensor video training. Overall, Retain is better suited to smaller tensors and greater computation concurrency, while Reweight is preferable for larger video tensors. Detailed schedule-selection guidance is provided in Appendix F . Figure 6: GPU memory scaling with selected timesteps on FLUX.1-dev and Wan2.2-TI2V-5B. 6 Conclusion We presented LeanGRPO, an objective-preserving execution framework that eliminates redundant update-stage recomputation in on-policy trajectory-logprob diffusion RL. Its Retain and Reweight schedules expose complementary memory tradeoffs while preserving the original policy gradient. Across multiple algorithms and FLUX.1-dev, SD3.5, and Wan backbones, LeanGRPO preserves reward improvement and achieves end-to-end speedups of up to 1.83 × 1.83	imes . References Bergmeister et al. (2026) Andreas Bergmeister, Stefanie Jegelka, Nikolas Nüsken, Carles Domingo-Enrich, and Jakiw Pidstrigach. Reinforce adjoint matching: Scaling RL post-training of diffusion and flow-matching models, 2026. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10759 . Black et al. (2024) Kevin Black, Michael Janner, Yilun Du, Ilya Kostrikov, and Sergey Levine. Training diffusion models with reinforcement learning, 2024. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13301 . Choi et al. (2026) Jaemoo Choi, Yuchen Zhu, Wei Guo, Petr Molodyk, Bo Yuan, Jinbin Bai, Yi Xin, Molei Tao, and Yongxin Chen. Rethinking the design space of reinforcement learning for diffusion models: On the importance of likelihood estimation beyond loss design, 2026. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04663 . Deng et al. (2026) Haoyou Deng, Keyu Yan, Chaojie Mao, Xiang Wang, Yu Liu, Changxin Gao, and Nong Sang. DenseGRPO: From sparse